INT. VENDING MACHINE REPAIR DEPOT - WAREHOUSE FLOOR - MORNING

Rows of dead snack machines stand against the corrugated walls, dozens of glass faces glowing faintly with the light of nothing. A forklift idles in an aisle. The air smells of cold coffee and burnt wiring.

DELIA ACHTERBERG, forties, in an orange safety vest two sizes too big, perches on a stool among the machines with a clipboard on her knee. She has been counting the same row for an hour. Her pen taps once for each machine, a metronome of grievance.

The service door clangs open. Sunlight cuts across the floor. NORBERT PIMM, fifties, regional quality manager, enters in a pressed suit and shiny brogues, carrying a leather folio and the faint air of a man who has never once been in a warehouse. He stops. He sees the machines. He sees Delia. He smiles.

NORBERT
Delia! There you are.

DELIA
(not looking up)
Here I am.

NORBERT
I come bearing news.

DELIA
You always do.

Norbert advances through the aisle, picking his way between puddles of oil, and sets the folio on a machine labelled OUT OF ORDER. Delia lifts her pen and sets it down on the clipboard without a sound. Norbert opens the folio, closes it, opens it again, and places a single sheet of paper in her lap.

NORBERT (CONT'D)
Your position has been reclassified.

DELIA
To what?

NORBERT
Synergy Associate.

DELIA
That's not a job.

NORBERT
It's a journey.

She stands. The clipboard slides to the floor. She takes a step toward him and he takes one back, bumping a machine, which dispenses a lone packet of crisps with a mechanical sigh. Both of them look at it in silence.

DELIA
(quietly)
Eleven years, Norbert.

NORBERT
And we value every one of them.

She picks up the crisps. Tears the packet. Eats one, slowly, looking at him, and drops the empty bag at his feet.

DELIA
That machine has been broken since 2019.

NORBERT
Then it's an exciting time for it.

Delia laughs, a low terrible sound, and then she is not laughing, and the forklift beeps in the distance like a heart monitor in the quiet.
